      "query": "# Deep Research Investigation: Will AI Create More Jobs Than It Destroys by 2030?\n\nConduct a comprehensive, evidence-based research investigation into how artificial intelligence will affect global employment by 2030.\n\n## 1. Research objectives\n\nInvestigate:\n\n1. How many jobs are expected to be created and displaced by AI and related technological changes by 2030?\n2. Which industries and occupations are most exposed to automation?\n3. Which new occupations and skills are emerging?\n4. How will AI affect employment in developed countries versus developing countries?\n5. Will AI primarily replace workers, augment their productivity, or create entirely new jobs?\n6. What are the biggest uncertainties in current employment forecasts?\n\n## 2. Source requirements\n\nFind and examine at least 15 unique, relevant sources.\n\nPrioritize:\n- World Economic Forum\n- International Labour Organization\n- International Monetary Fund\n- OECD\n- World Bank\n- McKinsey Global Institute\n- Peer-reviewed academic research\n- Government labour statistics\n\nUse primary sources wherever possible.\n\nFor every source, provide:\n- Exact report or research title\n- Author or publishing organization\n- Publication date\n- Direct working URL\n- Relevant findings\n- Whether the source is primary research or secondary analysis\n\nDo not invent publications, authors, statistics, URLs, or citations.\n\n## 3. Mandatory investigation\n\nFind and verify the World Economic Forum's *Future of Jobs Report 2025*.\n\nVerify the report's actual estimates of:\n- Jobs created by 2030\n- Jobs displaced by 2030\n- Net employment change\n\nExtract these figures from the original report, not from unrelated articles.\n\nIf the report cannot be accessed, explain what you attempted and distinguish access failure from evidence that the report does not exist.\n\n## 4. Quantitative analysis\n\nCreate a table containing at least 12 verified statistics.\n\nInclude:\n- Statistic\n- Value\n- Year or forecast period\n- Country or geographical coverage\n- Original source\n- Direct URL\n\nClearly distinguish observed historical data from projections.\n\nDo not combine estimates that use different definitions or methodologies without explaining the differences.\n\n## 5. Contradictory evidence\n\nFind at least three credible sources that disagree about AI's employment impact.\n\nExplain:\n- What each source predicts\n- Why their estimates differ\n- Differences in methodology and assumptions\n- Which conclusions are most strongly supported\n\nDo not artificially manufacture disagreement.\n\n## 6. Case studies\n\nInvestigate at least four real organizations or industries that have experienced AI-related employment changes.\n\nFor each case study, distinguish:\n- Confirmed job losses\n- Hiring changes\n- Productivity improvements\n- AI-related restructuring\n- Claims that are reported but not independently verified\n\nDo not automatically attribute every layoff to AI.\n\n## 7. Final analysis\n\nProvide:\n- Executive summary\n- Research methodology\n- Findings by industry\n- Global and regional comparison\n- Verified statistics table\n- Case studies\n- Contradictory evidence\n- Limitations and uncertainties\n- Final conclusion\n\n## 8. Quality and verification rules\n\n- Use at least 15 unique sources.\n- Every important factual claim must have an identifiable citation.\n- Citations must link to the actual supporting source.\n- Never invent citations or statistics.\n- Deduplicate identical reports and alternate PDF/HTML versions.\n- Clearly label unsupported claims.\n- If a source cannot be accessed, do not pretend to have read it.\n- Do not use search-result snippets as substitutes for reading original reports.\n- Maintain a research evidence log.\n- If research tools fail or hit rate limits, save progress and resume rather than inventing results.\n\n## 9. Final deliverable\n\nWrite a detailed research report of approximately 2,500–3,500 words.\n\nFinish with a bibliography containing all sources, direct URLs, and publication dates.\n\nThe report must be based on verifiable evidence, not plausible-sounding AI-generated claims.",
